analysis:
Examined docs/provider/cli-reference.md. The only change (range_map_complete=true) is head line 1960. It adds `DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE_SSD_INCREMENTAL_CHECKPOINTS` to the LaunchAgent passthrough variable table and cites SSDPrefixCachePolicy.swift (`incrementalCheckpoints`). Per the row, `0`/`false`/`no`/`off` writes complete checkpoints instead of deltas.

The head line 1960 itself was not in the supplied head excerpt; head lines 1-396 and base lines 1727-2036 were reviewed. Base units 6 and 7 are unchanged context: the report-command evidence bounds, paths, the passthrough table and runtime constants.

Threat relevance is T-041 (SSD prefix-cache at-rest surface, SEC-035). The doc change describes incremental (delta) checkpoint writes in the SSD tier. The documented setting is a format choice, not a gate change to DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE, TTL, HMAC naming or AAD. It adds a daemon-forwarded variable, but the operator already controls their own environment, so this is not a new adversarial surface in the docs.

Integration questions:
- If this PR also changes SSDPrefixCachePolicy/SSDBlockStore to add delta checkpoints, verify the following against T-041 (c)/(d) and the detection_hint:
  - delta records keep per-file DEK/AES-GCM with canonical AAD;
  - no prompt-derived metadata beyond the keyed lookupTag (e.g. parent/base references must be HMAC tags);
  - cacheSalt is folded into delta chain lookups;
  - TTL/LRU eviction of a base invalidates dependent deltas (fail closed to recompute);
  - the disk budget accounts for delta chains;
  - LaunchAgent.swift passthrough actually includes the key.
- The threat model may need a note on the delta checkpoint format.

No source for these was supplied, so this is a coverage limit, not evidence of safety.
